
=== Microsoft Fabric Warehouse

ifndef::pro[]

Microsoft Fabric Warehouse is only supported in the professional version of SymmetricDS.

endif::pro[]

ifdef::pro[]

Microsoft Fabric Warehouse is supported as a write-only target.  See <<Write-Only Node>> for details on setting up a write-only node in SymmetricDS.
It uses the https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/connect/jdbc/download-microsoft-jdbc-driver-for-sql-server[Microsoft JDBC driver].

==== Setup

Set up the node by using the <<Set Up New Replication,Configure Diagram>> or the <<Add Node,Connect Database>> wizard and selecting Microsoft Fabric
Warehouse as the type.  Use the full host name of the warehouse for the endpoint and the name of the warehouse for the database name. The following is
an example of the JDBC URL:

----
jdbc:sqlserver://<workspace>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com:1433;database={<warehouse_name>};sendTimeAsDateTime=false;encrypt=true;trustServerCertificate=false;authentication=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal
----

The `sendTimeAsDateTime=false` property is required.  Without it the JDBC driver sends a `TIME` value as a `DATETIME`, which the warehouse cannot compare
with a `TIME` column, so an update or delete that compares a `TIME` column fails with the error "The data types time and datetime are incompatible in the
equal to operator".  The web console adds the property to the example URL.

Microsoft Fabric Warehouse accepts only Microsoft Entra ID authentication.  SymmetricDS uses the service principal of an Entra app registration.
The credentials are entered in the User Id and Password fields:

[horizontal]
User Id:: The Application (client) ID of the app registration
Password:: A client secret of the app registration

WARNING: The connection string shown by the Fabric portal uses `Authentication=ActiveDirectoryInteractive`.  This mode needs to open a browser for
sign-in whenever it opens a connection, which is not compatible with SymmetricDS.  Use `Authentication=ActiveDirectoryServicePrincipal` instead.

The `fabric` module contains the Microsoft Authentication Library that is required for Entra ID authentication and the libraries that bulk loading uses to
upload files to OneLake.  The web console downloads it when the connection is tested, or you can run `symadmin module install fabric` to install it.

==== Permissions

The app registration needs to be granted access in each of the following steps:

. Create an app registration in Microsoft Entra ID and a client secret for it.  Depending on the tenant settings, service principals might need to be
allowed to use Fabric APIs, which is a tenant setting in the Fabric admin portal.
. Add the app registration to the workspace that contains the warehouse with the Contributor role.  Sharing the warehouse item is not enough, and the
personal "My workspace" cannot grant roles, so use a standard workspace.
. Grant the app registration the database permissions that SymmetricDS needs.  The `CREATE USER` statement is not supported by a warehouse.  The database
user is created when the first `GRANT` or `ALTER ROLE` statement runs.  Connect to the warehouse as an administrator and run the following script,
replacing `<app registration name>` with the display name of the app registration:

[source, Sql]
----
ALTER ROLE db_ddladmin ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
ALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
ALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [<app registration name>];
----

==== Bulk Loading

SymmetricDS can bulk load a warehouse with the `COPY INTO` statement.  When a channel uses the bulk data loader, which the reload channel does by default,
SymmetricDS writes the rows of a batch to a CSV file, uploads the file to a lakehouse in OneLake, loads it with `COPY INTO`, and deletes the file.

Bulk loading is used when the following parameters are set.  Until they are, data is loaded using JDBC batch statements.  The web console asks for these
values when the node is added.  The tenant, workspace and lakehouse IDs must be GUIDs.  If one of them is set to something else, the web console doesn't
accept it, and a node that was configured by other means logs a warning that names the parameter and loads with JDBC batch statements.

[horizontal]
cloud.bulk.load.onelake.tenant.id:: The Directory (tenant) ID of the Microsoft Entra tenant that owns the app registration
cloud.bulk.load.onelake.workspace.id:: The ID of the workspace that contains the lakehouse
cloud.bulk.load.onelake.lakehouse.id:: The ID of the lakehouse where the files are staged
cloud.bulk.load.onelake.folder:: The folder under *Files* in the lakehouse where the files are staged.  The default is `symmetricds`.

Enter the IDs, which are GUIDs instead of the names because OneLake doesn't accept a name that contains a space.  SymmetricDS signs in to OneLake with
the node's `target.db.user` and `target.db.password`, so the app registration needs access to the lakehouse as well as to the warehouse.
The workspace role in the Permissions section applies to every item in the workspace.

To find the values:

[horizontal]
Tenant ID:: In the Microsoft Entra admin center, open the app registration and copy the *Directory (tenant) ID* from the Overview page.
Workspace ID and lakehouse ID:: In the Fabric portal, open the lakehouse.  The address of the page has the form
`+https://app.fabric.microsoft.com/groups/<workspace ID>/lakehouses/<lakehouse ID>+`.

WARNING: Do not copy the values from the host name of the warehouse endpoint, `<tenant>-<workspace>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com`.  Its two parts are
shortened encodings of the tenant ID and the workspace ID, not GUIDs, and neither is the ID of a lakehouse.  OneLake rejects them with the error "Required
item type extension is missing in the item name".

Each batch of a table is loaded with its own `COPY INTO` statement, which has a fixed cost.  Larger batches therefore are more efficient.  Set the max
batch size of a channel that uses the bulk loader accordingly.  Because each batch is uploaded to OneLake over the network, the speed of the connection
between SymmetricDS and OneLake also affects how long a load takes.

A table with an identity column that SymmetricDS didn't create is loaded with `IDENTITY_INSERT` turned on for the `COPY INTO` statement, so the identity
values of the source are kept.  SymmetricDS doesn't reseed the identity column afterward.  If rows are also inserted into the table outside of
SymmetricDS, run `DBCC CHECKIDENT ('<table>', RESEED)` after a load so that the values the warehouse generates don't collide with the loaded values.  A
warehouse doesn't accept a new value for `RESEED`, and the values it generates are unique but not sequential, so they can be much larger than the values of
the source.

If SymmetricDS stops while it uploads or loads a file, the file might be left in the `cloud.bulk.load.onelake.folder`, and it can be deleted.

==== Known Limitations

Microsoft Fabric Warehouse supports a subset of the SQL Server features, so SymmetricDS adjusts the tables that it creates.

===== Constraints

Primary key, foreign key, and unique constraints cannot be part of a `CREATE TABLE` statement.  SymmetricDS creates the table and then adds each
constraint with an `ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT` statement that ends in `NOT ENFORCED`.  A warehouse never enforces these constraints, so inserting
a duplicate key succeeds.  Indexes other than unique constraints are not created.

To leave the primary keys out of the tables that are created on the target, set the `create.table.without.primary.keys` parameter to `true` on the node
that sends the table definition.  The parameter applies to every target of that node.  Updates and deletes are still applied by the primary key columns
of the source table as long as the `dataloader.use.primary.keys.from.source` parameter is `true`.  If it is set to `false`, all columns of a table
without a primary key are used to find the row.  A warehouse rejects a foreign key whose referenced columns are not a primary key or a unique constraint,
so when you set `create.table.without.primary.keys` to `true`, it is recommended to also set `create.table.without.foreign.keys` to `true`.

===== Other Unsupported Features

* Default values are not created.
* Computed columns are not created.
* Triggers are not supported.
* Identity columns are not created.  SymmetricDS loads the values from the source.

===== Data Types

The following data types are not supported by a warehouse.  SymmetricDS creates the replacement type instead:

[cols="1,2"]
|===
|Source type |Type created

|`NVARCHAR(n)`
|`VARCHAR(n * 3)`, or `VARCHAR(MAX)` when that is more than 8000

|`NCHAR(n)`
|`CHAR(n * 3)`, or `VARCHAR(MAX)` when that is more than 8000

|`NVARCHAR(MAX)`, `NCLOB`, `LONGNVARCHAR`, `XML`, `SQL_VARIANT`
|`VARCHAR(MAX)`

|`BINARY(n)`
|`VARBINARY(n)`

|`TINYINT`
|`SMALLINT`

|`TIMESTAMP` and `TIME` with a precision above 6
|`DATETIME2(6)` and `TIME(6)`

|`TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE`, `DATETIMEOFFSET`
|`DATETIME2(6)`
|===

The length of a `VARCHAR` column is counted in bytes of UTF-8 text instead of characters, which is why the length of a Unicode column is tripled.  The
length of a `VARCHAR` column that is not a Unicode column is copied as it is, so it may need to be increased when the source stores single-byte characters
that need more than one byte in UTF-8.

WARNING: A time zone offset cannot be stored in a `DATETIME2` column.  The offset is not kept, and the value that is stored depends on the conversion
that is done by the warehouse.

NOTE: A `SQL_VARIANT` value is sent together with its base type, precision and scale, and SymmetricDS only removes them when the target column is a
`SQL_VARIANT`.  The warehouse column is `VARCHAR(MAX)`, so the value is stored as text in the form `value|basetype|precision|scale`.

===== Changing a Table

A warehouse can only add nullable columns, widen the size of a column, change a column from `NOT NULL` to `NULL`, and drop a column.  Any other
change, such as making a column `NOT NULL`, making a column smaller, or changing its data type, is done by re-creating the table.  SymmetricDS copies the
data into a table named `<table>_`, drops the table, creates it again, copies the data back and then drops the copy.  A warning is logged.

The table is re-created in a single transaction.  If any step fails, for example because a column that was made `NOT NULL` contains `null`s, the transaction
is rolled back.  The table keeps its original definition and its rows, and no `<table>_` table is left behind.  The batch fails with the error from the
warehouse and is retried until the cause is fixed, for example by removing the `null`s.  Adding a column that is `NOT NULL` and has no default value also
re-creates the table, but the existing rows are not copied back.  They remain in the `<table>_` table.

endif::pro[]
